  "slug": "scientific-fair-5429",
  "summary": "Time-resolved single-cell and spatial transcriptomics now support learning interaction and developmental dynamics from unmatched snapshots and trajectories.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is inferring dynamical laws of cell behavior from snapshot or time-resolved single-cell and spatial measurements, using stochastic, neural SDE, or optimal transport formulations. Several items target interaction terms directly: a many-body stochastic framework learns cell-cell interaction laws, including nonreciprocal couplings, from experimental trajectories, and stMIND infers multiscale spatial-temporal interactions from unmatched time-resolved spatial transcriptomics. Others reconstruct continuous dynamics from snapshots (CytoBridge, via an unbalanced mean-field Schrödinger bridge) or fit developmental vector fields and compare lineage paths with minimum-action routes (LADD). scJDO2 estimates local score and flow Jacobian operators and examines what such operators can and cannot identify. PRISM addresses simulator choice for multi-fidelity inference of epithelial vertex models. The newer element is the explicit focus on identifiability and calibration of inferred dynamics.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-11"
}
